"Identical twins can look so much alike that even their friends mix them up. Why? Both of them grew from the same single cell, so both carry almost exactly the same set of instructions. Those instructions are written in a molecule called DNA. DNA is very long and very thin, and it is shaped like a ladder that has been twisted. Scientists call this shape a double helix. The two sides of the ladder are long strands. The rungs between them are made of four small chemical parts, which we call by their first letters: A, T, C, and G. The letters follow one simple rule. A always pairs with T, and C always pairs with G. So each rung is a pair, and if you know the letters along one side of the ladder, you can work out the letters along the other side. This rule is what lets a cell copy its DNA. Before a cell divides, the ladder unzips down the middle. Each loose letter picks up a new partner, A with T and C with G, until there are two ladders where there was one. They read the same, and each has one old strand and one new strand. The order of the letters is the message. A stretch of DNA that carries the instructions for one job is called a gene. People have about 19,000 to 20,000 genes that tell cells how to build proteins. One full set of human DNA has about 3.1 billion pairs of letters, and it is packed into 23 pairs of bundles called chromosomes. Now and then a letter is changed. This is called a mutation. Many mutations make no difference at all, and some change how a living thing is built. Every living thing we know, from a mushroom to an orchid to you, writes its instructions with the same four letters. Only the order is different.": "e150e0130f66",
